{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"NIH funded about 1,924 fewer grants through Aug 2026 than at the same point in FY25, even though dollars obligated are near the full budget.","detail":"AAMC's brief (data through 31 Aug 2026, published 9 Sep 2026) reports $30.2B obligated, about 89% of the ~$34B extramural budget. It also reports 1,924 fewer grants than at the same point in FY25 and 9,714 fewer than FY24. Dollars are roughly intact but spread over fewer awards, so fewer labs have grants to spend on consumables. This quantifies the 'tight funding' point at the unit level. The ledger has F383 (~3,000 fewer projects) but not this monthly count.","url":"https://www.aamc.org/about-us/biomedical-research/publication/tracking-nih-awards-fy-2026-2","date":"2026-09-09","source_name":"AAMC Tracking NIH Awards FY2026 (data through 31 Aug)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"1,924 fewer grants than at the same point in FY 2025","strength":"medium","why_new":"Gives the AAMC Aug-2026 award-count gap vs FY25 and FY24 as a dated series point; the known file only has aggregate NIH obligation and award shares."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"The FY24 award-count gap has stayed near 9,000-10,000 all year, so there has been no catch-up.","detail":"The gap vs FY24 is 'nearly 9,000' at 30 Jun, about 10,000 at 31 Jul and 9,714 at 31 Aug. The gap is not closing even as obligations jump from $18B to $30.2B. The FY26 award base is therefore structurally about 10K awards smaller than FY24. That fits a research-customer base that is spending less on consumables than in FY24.","url":"https://www.aamc.org/about-us/biomedical-research/publication/tracking-nih-awards-fy-2026-1","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"AAMC Tracking NIH Awards FY2026 (Jun/Jul/Aug briefs)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"approximately 10,000 fewer grants compared to this point in FY 2024","strength":"medium","why_new":"Builds the Jun/Jul/Aug monthly series of the gap to FY24 (~9.0K / ~10.0K / 9.7K), which is not in the known file."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"Competitive new-award activity is down sharply: through 31 Jul 2026, competitive R01s were down 14% and competitive research project grants (RPGs) down 8% vs FY24.","detail":"The AAMC brief with data through 31 Jul 2026 (published 5 Aug) gives these as comparisons to the same point in FY24. R01s are the bread-and-butter investigator grants that fund reagent purchases. The 14% fall in competitive R01s is steeper than the 8% fall in all competitive RPGs, which shows the damage sits in the core academic grant type. The brief gives no FY25 comparison.","url":"https://www.aamc.org/about-us/biomedical-research/publication/tracking-nih-awards-fy-2026-1","date":"2026-08-05","source_name":"AAMC Tracking NIH Awards FY2026 (data through 31 Jul)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"The known file has a secondary 'R01-equivalents -24% y/y' claim; this is the primary AAMC count vs FY24 (-14% R01, -8% RPG)."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"Multiyear (up-front) funding of competitive RPGs rose from $769M (30 Jun) to $1.1B (31 Jul) to $1.6B (31 Aug), vs only $556.4M at 31 Jul FY25.","detail":"At 31 Jul the FY26 figure was about 2x the FY25 figure at the same date ($1.1B vs $556.4M). By 31 Aug it was $1.6B. Each up-front dollar pays for several years of work at once, which crowds out new awards and cuts the number of labs with fresh money for reagents. This is the likely mechanism behind the fewer-grants-but-full-dollars pattern, and it argues for weak new-project reagent demand into 2027. It extends F384, which only noted the crowd-out qualitatively.","url":"https://www.aamc.org/about-us/biomedical-research/publication/tracking-nih-awards-fy-2026-2","date":"2026-09-09","source_name":"AAMC Tracking NIH Awards FY2026","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Gives the three-month MYF dollar series and the FY25 comparator, not previously quantified."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"against","claim":"NIH obligations are catching up in dollars: $18B at 30 Jun to $30.2B at 31 Aug, 89% of the ~$34B extramural budget.","detail":"Cumulative obligations went from $18B (53% of $34B) to $23.3B (69%) to $30.2B (89%). The implied monthly obligations are about $5.3B in July and $6.9B in August, though the brief itself states $6.6B for August (the two don't reconcile). Only $3.7B remains for September. At 30 Jun the total was above FY25 but $2.6B below FY24. So the dollar flow is large and should reach labs by year-end. That undercuts a funding-cliff argument for Q4'26, though not for award counts.","url":"https://www.aamc.org/about-us/biomedical-research/publication/tracking-nih-awards-fy-2026-2","date":"2026-09-09","source_name":"AAMC Tracking NIH Awards FY2026","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Builds the cumulative-dollar monthly series (18.0 / 23.3 / 30.2) with percent of budget; the known file only has the Mar-2026 $5.8B/34%-below-FY24 point."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"against","claim":"The NIH catch-up ($5.3B in July, $6.6B in August) means a big wave of award money reached institutions in Q3'26, which could support a Q4'26 research consumables rebound.","detail":"Thermo says funding takes 2-3 quarters to reach spend (known). Heavy obligations from July to August 2026 would therefore flow into lab spending from roughly Q4'26 to Q1'27. This runs against Illumina's FY26 guide of a mid- to high-single-digit research decline with no recovery assumed. It is a watch-out for Q4'26 R&A and for the 2027 base (research consumables flat to up), rather than a refutation of price pressure.","url":"https://www.aamc.org/about-us/biomedical-research/publication/tracking-nih-awards-fy-2026-2","date":"2026-09-09","source_name":"AAMC Tracking NIH Awards FY2026","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Links the dated obligation surge to the lag-to-spend mechanism and the Q4'26 R&A guide; the known file does not contain Jul/Aug obligations."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"The comparison to FY25 is weak: through 30 Jun 2026 obligations only 'surpass' FY25, and by 31 Aug the grant count was still 1,924 below FY25.","detail":"FY25 was the year Illumina's R&A fell about 7% on a US academic cut it had assumed at -15%. FY26 is still below FY25 in grant count, so FY26 NIH-funded activity is no better than that low base. A mid- to high-single-digit R&A decline in 2026 is consistent with this. A 2027 recovery needs award counts to rebound, and nothing in the AAMC series shows that yet.","url":"https://www.aamc.org/about-us/biomedical-research/publication/tracking-nih-awards-fy-2026","date":"2026-07-07","source_name":"AAMC Tracking NIH Awards FY2026 (30 Jun brief)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Tests the R&A guide directly against the primary award series rather than secondary commentary."},
